save(i,dict(title='Polynomial bit size of short Stabbing Planes proofs',formal='Historical question, resolved affirmatively: is there a universal polynomial p such that, for every n≥1, every unsatisfiable CNF formula F on n Boolean variables and every Stabbing Planes refutation of F with ℓ tree nodes, F also has a Stabbing Planes refutation of total binary encoding length at most p(n+ℓ)? The original refutation may use integer coefficients of arbitrarily large magnitude. The replacement may change the tree and use more nodes; neither the original depth nor the original queried inequalities must be preserved. Dadush and Tiwari’s 2020 recompilation theorem answers this question.',definitions='A CNF formula is a conjunction of clauses, each a disjunction of literals xᵢ or ¬xᵢ. Repeated literals may be deleted and tautological clauses discarded. Its real relaxation K_F⊆[0,1]ⁿ consists of points satisfying ∑ᵢ∈P xᵢ+∑ⱼ∈N(1−xⱼ)≥1 for each clause with positive-variable set P and negative-variable set N, together with 0≤xᵢ≤1 for every i. Unsatisfiable means K_F∩{0,1}ⁿ is empty; K_F itself may contain fractional points. An SP refutation is a finite rooted full binary tree. At each internal node choose a∈ℤⁿ and b∈ℤ; the two outgoing edges add a·x≥b and a·x≤b−1 respectively. At a leaf, the inequalities of K_F together with the branch inequalities on its root-to-leaf path must be infeasible over ℝ. The discarded open slab b−1<a·x<b has no integer points, which makes the branching sound. Each leaf includes an exact certificate of infeasibility: a finite list of input or path inequalities, written in ≥ form, and nonnegative rational multipliers whose weighted sum is 0≥1. Coefficients and constants are encoded in signed binary; rational multipliers use binary integer numerators and positive denominators. Tree topology, every branch vector and threshold, and the leaf certificates are all charged to proof bit size. Input axioms used at a leaf can be specified by their clause or variable-bound encodings; the entire input formula is not counted anew as part of the proof. The encoding uses no arithmetic circuits or other compressed representations for large integers. Length ℓ counts all tree nodes; internal-node or leaf counts are polynomially equivalent conventions. Depth is the maximum number of edges on a root-to-leaf path and is a separate measure. The same input F and variables must be refuted. The target is existence of a small encoded replacement proof, not a polynomial-time construction measured only in n and ℓ when the original proof itself may require far more bits to read.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='The affirmative resolution is a uniform polynomial bound on the encoded replacement refutation, including rational leaf certificates, for every CNF refutation of length ℓ. The published theorem gives at most O(nℓ) nodes with polynomial-bit branch coefficients for K_F⊆[0,1]ⁿ, and the accompanying certificate analysis supplies a polynomial total size. For arbitrary rational input systems, the certified-size bound must also account for the input system’s encoding length. A small bit length must not be confused with polynomially bounded numerical coefficient magnitude.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Two proof measures needed reconciliation',text='A branching tree can have few nodes while one inequality uses an enormous integer. The 2018 article therefore distinguished the number of nodes from the total bits needed to encode a proof, and asked whether that distinction can be removed at polynomial precision.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The published recompilation theorem answers yes',text='For an integer-free compact convex set inside an ℓ₁ ball of radius R, Theorem 1.1 gives a new branching proof with O(nℓ) nodes and coefficients, including thresholds, of magnitude at most (10nR)^{(n+2)²}. For a Boolean CNF relaxation one may take R=n, giving polynomial bit length in n. The theorem explicitly identifies the earlier SP question as resolved.',citation='solution'),dict(title='Leaf certificates and general inputs matter',text='Corollary 1.2 gives a certified proof of total size O(n⁶Lℓ) for a rational polytope whose defining system has encoding length L. Its discussion bounds certificates using small infeasible subsystems. The later Stabbing Planes text explicitly records polynomial equivalence for CNF formulas and suitably bounded inequality systems, rather than removing dependence on arbitrarily large input coefficients.',citation='updated'),dict(title='A polynomial number of bits still permits very large coefficients',text='The normalization does not place general SP inside the substantially smaller-magnitude coefficient restrictions used in some simulations by Cutting Planes. The updated source treats the length-versus-size resolution separately from those simulations and their lower-bound consequences.',citation='updated')],why='Justifies counting branching nodes as a robust proof-complexity measure for Boolean Stabbing Planes, without hiding an uncontrolled amount of information in coefficients. It also clarifies the relationship between geometric branching and finite, checkable proof encodings.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2018',text='The source separates length from encoded size and asks whether every short SP refutation has a polynomial-bit replacement.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2020-07-17',text='CCC publication resolves the coefficient question in Theorem 1.1 and provides certified bit-size bounds in Corollary 1.2.',citation='solution'),dict(date='2023-03-17',text='The original authors’ revised full text explicitly records the resolution and includes the certified-size statement as Proposition 4.',citation='updated')],status='resolved',status_note='Read the original open passage and proof definitions, Dadush–Tiwari Theorem 1.1 and Corollary 1.2 with their certificate and input-size discussion, and the original authors’ March 2023 revision acknowledging the resolution. Matched CNF Boolean relaxation, polynomial versus numerical coefficient bounds, node overhead and leaf certification. Kept the input-length dependence for general rational systems. No polynomial-time-in-node-count conversion or independent proof audit is claimed.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='Establishes that a short geometric branching proof can be represented with polynomially many bits, validating a fundamental complexity measure for this proof system. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Recovered the node-count versus bit-size question and exact proof model; marked resolved using the 2020 theorem and the 2023 source revision’s explicit acknowledgment.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['Stabbing Planes refutes Boolean formulas by branching on integer linear inequalities.','A short tree can still appear expensive to encode if its coefficients are enormous.','Dadush and Tiwari showed that such proofs can be replaced by proofs with polynomial-bit coefficients and only O(n) more nodes per original node.','Leaf infeasibility certificates can also be kept within polynomial encoded size for CNF inputs.','The original authors’ 2023 revision explicitly records the resolution; polynomial bit size does not mean polynomial coefficient magnitude.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','solution','updated'])),notes=['Recovered CNF relaxation, arbitrary integer branches, real infeasibility leaves and full binary proof encoding.','Matched Theorem 1.1 and certified Corollary 1.2, keeping general-system input bit length.','Confirmed resolution in original authors’ 2023 Proposition 4 and Subsequent Work section.','Separated polynomial coefficient bit length from the much smaller numerical bounds used in SP* results.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.04124'])
